  "finding_count": 3,
  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "Proposed pair (frozen for the sealed evaluation); the pair was chosen on the development partition and then evaluated once on the sealed partitions",
      "evidence": "paper_data.json final_evaluation.program_tree_sha256 (sha256:3f76de56...) is not reproduced by sha256sum of source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py (62f9cd0a...) or config_latent_ext/program.py (d68d9520...); analysis/search_metrics.json entries 02 and 03 have identical metrics and seed scores; paper.tex Supplementary Information says the hash could not be matched; paper_data.json evaluation_evidence has candidate_diagnostics_trust=unverified.",
      "fix": "State in the main text (Compared approaches and Method) that the sealed run used either the proposed pair or its latent-skill extension, and that the available evidence cannot say which. Remove or qualify \"frozen for the sealed evaluation\" and \"evaluated once\". Make the abstract and conclusion refer to the evaluated selector-imputer family rather than asserting the exact described pair.",
      "id": "sealed-config-identity-1",
      "issue": "The evidence does not establish that the sealed evaluation ran the described proposed pair. The development scores of the proposed pair and the latent-extension pair tie exactly, and the paper's own Supplementary Information says the evaluated source-tree hash could not be matched to a single source directory. The main text still states the identity of the frozen configuration as fact and says \"evaluated once\", which no artifact records. The abstract and conclusion attribute the sealed numbers to the stack described in the Method.",
      "section": "Method (Compared approaches), Discussion (Threats to validity 4), Abstract/Conclusion"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Table tab:hyper constants are read from the entry-point source of the evaluated configuration (src/program.py)",
      "evidence": "source_evidence/config_proposed/program.py holds the table values (EM_SHRINK=0.02, STACK_TAUS, CANDIDATES, budgets 70/150/210). config_latent_ext/program.py is a different file and the tree hash is unmatched, per the paper_data.json final_evaluation record.",
      "fix": "Reword the table caption and lead-in to say the constants come from the proposed-pair source copy, and that this copy is not verified as the exact sealed-evaluated tree.",
      "id": "si-hyperparameters-source-1",
      "issue": "The constants were read from the config_proposed copy, whose match to the sealed-evaluated configuration is unverified. The same section says the evaluated hash does not match any source directory. The table is therefore presented as the evaluated configuration's constants without the needed qualification.",
      "section": "Supplementary Information (Method specification and hyperparameters)"
    },
    {
      "claim": "H3 is supported only weakly (a 0.016 skill margin over random-k ridge)",
      "evidence": "claims_grounding.json claim on skill 0.099 vs 0.016; paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.final_skill_vs_random_selection=0.0160; paper.tex Results (Selection versus imputation; What a different outcome would have looked like) and Discussion (Uncertainty).",
      "fix": "Change the Introduction to say H3 is not tested by the sealed evaluation, because selector and imputer are scored jointly. Report the 0.016 margin as small and unresolved, consistent with the Results and Discussion.",
      "id": "h3-verdict-inconsistent-1",
      "issue": "The Introduction counts the 0.016 margin as weak support for H3 (selector adds accuracy beyond random selection). The Results section says the sealed metrics do not isolate selection and that H3 is \"not tested sharply\". The Discussion says the margin should be viewed as indistinguishable from zero and makes no significance claim. The only retained selection-only evidence points the other way: skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-k ridge, so the greedy baseline did no better than random.",
      "section": "Introduction (What the evidence supports) vs Results and Discussion"
    }
  ],
